## 2. Mathematical Baseline

### 2.1 The Impedance Match Theorem
$$\text{human}_1 \neq \text{math}_1$$

Humans express intent through ambiguous, high-entropy natural language sequences. Machines require rigid, low-entropy structural payloads. The engine acts as a **cognitive impedance matcher**, dampening entropy through token intersection scoring.

### 2.2 Deterministic Origin Seed
An event origin $O$ is defined as the tuple:

$$O = \text{SHA256}(\text{UUID}_v4 \parallel t_{\text{UTC}} \parallel \text{GPS}_{\text{lat,long}} \parallel \text{Entropy}_{\text{local}})$$

Where:
- $\text{UUID}_v4$: Universally unique identity token.
- $t_{\text{UTC}}$: Monotonic timestamp synchronized to UTC atomic reference.
- $\text{GPS}_{\text{lat,long}}$: Physical spatial coordinate vector.
- $\text{Entropy}_{\text{local}}$: Local hardware noise or cryptographic nonce.

This guarantees that every routed intent or state change is cryptographically tied to an exact physical time and place on the infinite plane.

